    "name":  "Alcoholic cirrhosis of liver with ascites",
    "module":  "hepatobiliary",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Alcoholic cirrhosis with ascites represents decompensated cirrhosis — a critical prognostic inflection point with a 2-year survival of approximately 50%. Ascites develops when portal hypertension and reduced hepatic albumin synthesis impair fluid homeostasis, leading to peritoneal accumulation. Complications include spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP), hepatorenal syndrome, and hepatic encephalopathy. All patients with new-onset ascites should undergo diagnostic paracentesis to calculate SAAG (\u003e1.1 g/dL confirms portal hypertension) and exclude SBP. Treatment includes sodium restriction, diuretics (spironolactone ± furosemide), and regular large-volume paracentesis for refractory cases.",
